@@ -3,3 +3,45 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## Building blocks
+
+### JSON Pointer — `resolvePointer(document, pointer)`
+
+Resolves a [JSON Pointer](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901) against a JSON document and returns
+the value it refers to, by reference. The document is never modified.
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { foo: ['bar', 'baz'], 'a/b': 1 };
+
+resolvePointer(document, '/foo/1'); // 'baz'
+resolvePointer(document, '/a~1b'); // 1
+resolvePointer(document, ''); // the whole document
+```
+
+It throws instead of returning a fallback, so a missing value is never confused with a value that is
+`null`:
+
+- `InvalidPointerError` — the string is not a JSON Pointer: it is non-empty and does not start with
+  `/`, or it contains a `~` that is not followed by `0` or `1`. The URI fragment representation of
+  RFC 6901, section 6 (`#/foo`) is a different form and is rejected too.
+- `PointerResolutionError` — the pointer is valid, but the document has no value there: an object has
+  no such member, an array index is out of range or not an `array-index` (`-`, `01`, `length`), or the
+  pointer descends into a string, number, boolean or `null`.
+
+Both derive from `JsonPointerError`, so one `catch` covers either. Member lookup only considers a
+document's own members: `/toString` and `/__proto__` never resolve.
+
+## Working on the library
+
+```sh
+npm install
+npm test        # node --test, running the TypeScript sources directly
+npm run typecheck
+npm run build   # emits dist/
+```
+
+The sources are run as-is by Node's type stripping, which is why relative imports carry the `.ts`
+extension; `tsc` rewrites them to `.js` when it emits `dist/`.
