+---
+id: dl-001-resolvepointer-api-a-typed-jsonpointererror-hierarchy
+type: decision-log
+title: "resolvePointer API: a typed JsonPointerError hierarchy"
+status: pending
+---
+
+# resolvePointer API: a typed JsonPointerError hierarchy
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` has to signal two different kinds of failure, which RFC 6901
+itself keeps apart:
+
+- the string handed in is not a JSON Pointer at all (section 3, the grammar);
+- the string is a JSON Pointer, but the document has no value at the location it identifies
+  (section 4, the evaluation rules).
+
+The first is a programming or configuration error: the pointer is wrong wherever it came from, and no
+document will ever make it work. The second is data-dependent and routine: callers legitimately probe
+a document for a value that may be absent. JSON Pointer is the first of several building blocks, and
+the ones that follow (operations that read and update a document through pointers) need to tell the
+two apart — a bad pointer in a patch is a malformed request, a missing target is a different outcome.
+
+Returning `undefined` for "absent" was rejected: a JSON document may hold `null`, and a toolkit that
+conflates "no value there" with "the value is null" would push that ambiguity into every caller.
+
+## Decision
+
+`resolvePointer` throws, and `src/json-pointer.ts` exports a three-class hierarchy:
+
+- `JsonPointerError extends Error` — the base class, so one `catch` covers the module;
+- `InvalidPointerError extends JsonPointerError` — the grammar was not met;
+- `PointerResolutionError extends JsonPointerError` — the pointer does not resolve.
+
+Each class sets `name` to its own class name, so the kind survives serialisation to a log.
+
+Syntax is validated before the walk starts, so an invalid pointer is always reported as
+`InvalidPointerError` even when the document would not have resolved it either. The non-string
+argument check lives in the same place: the pointer is external input, and it is validated at the
+boundary.
+
+Messages are built to be read in a log without the call site at hand: they quote the whole pointer,
+name how far the walk got (`the document`, or `the value at "/a/b"`), and say what was wrong. The
+document's own values are never interpolated into a message, so a resolution failure cannot leak the
+content of a payload.
+
+The exported JSON data model (`JsonValue`, `JsonArray`, `JsonObject`, in `src/json-value.ts`) is
+shared rather than private to this module: the value a pointer resolves to is itself a document that
+the next building blocks will take as input.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers that only want "did it resolve" write one `try`/`catch` on `PointerResolutionError`;
+  callers that validate pointers up front catch `InvalidPointerError`.
+- The error classes are public API. Renaming one, or moving a condition from one class to the other,
+  is a breaking change for the services that import the toolkit.
+- `resolvePointer` is not a predicate. A caller that needs "does this pointer resolve?" without the
+  throw will want a companion helper; none is added until a caller needs it.
