+
+# JSON Pointer scope: strict RFC 6901, own members only
+
+## Context
+
+RFC 6901 is short, but it leaves a handful of points where an implementation can be lenient or
+strict, and JSON Pointer implementations in the wild differ on each of them. The toolkit is imported
+by several of the team's services, so a pointer has to mean the same thing everywhere; the README
+states that behaviour must follow the published standard. Each point below is therefore settled once,
+in favour of the RFC, and covered by a test.
+
+## Decision
+
+**The URI fragment form is not accepted.** Section 6 defines a second representation, for use inside
+a URI fragment, where the pointer is percent-encoded after a `#`. It is a different string with
+different escaping rules, and accepting both in one function would make `#` ambiguous: `#/foo` would
+stop being resolvable as the member named `#` of a nested object. `resolvePointer` implements section
+3 only; `#/foo` raises `InvalidPointerError`. A caller holding a fragment decodes it first. If that
+turns out to be common, it belongs in a separate, explicitly named function.
+
+**`-` on an array never resolves.** Section 4 gives `-` the meaning "the member after the last array
+element", and notes that using it to index an array "will always result in such an error condition".
+It is useful to the operations that *add* to an array, which will interpret it themselves; for
+resolution it is a `PointerResolutionError` with a message that says so, rather than the generic "not
+an array index".
+
+**An array index must match the `array-index` production.** `0`, or a non-zero digit followed by
+digits. So `/01`, `/+1`, `/1.0`, `/ 1` and `/length` do not resolve against an array, even though
+JavaScript would happily coerce or look some of them up.
+
+**Bad array indices are resolution errors, not syntax errors.** `array-index` belongs to the
+evaluation rules of section 4, not to the grammar of section 3: whether a token is an index at all
+depends on the document, since `/0` is a perfectly good member name for an object. A pointer is
+therefore judged valid without seeing the document, and only the walk can decide that `/01` leads
+nowhere.
+
+**Member lookup considers own members only.** The walk uses `Object.hasOwn`, so `/toString`,
+`/constructor`, `/__proto__` and anything inherited from a custom prototype do not resolve. A JSON
+document has no inherited members; resolving them would let a pointer reach JavaScript machinery that
+is not part of the data, which is both wrong and a prototype-pollution foothold for the operations
+that will later write through a pointer.
+
+**Escape sequences are resolved in one left-to-right pass**, which is what section 4 asks for when it
+notes that `~01` must become `~1` and not `/`. A `~` not followed by `0` or `1` is an
+`InvalidPointerError`; a `/` inside a token cannot occur, since `/` is the separator.
+
+**The empty token is a real member name.** The pointer `/` refers to the member named `""`, and `//`
+to the member `""` of that member. The pointer `""` refers to the whole document.
+
+**Unicode is not further validated.** The grammar's `unescaped` range is written in Unicode scalar
+values, which would exclude a lone surrogate. JavaScript strings, and `JSON.parse`, both admit lone
+surrogates, so rejecting them would make pointers unusable against documents the services already
+exchange. Every character other than an unescaped `~` is passed through as the member name.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A pointer that resolves here resolves to the same value in every service, and a pointer that is
+  lenient elsewhere (`#/foo`, `/01`) fails loudly rather than resolving to something unexpected.
+- Callers migrating from a lenient implementation may see new errors. That is the intent; the message
+  names the pointer and the location so the fix is local.
+- The operations built on top of this one inherit the strict reading, and must give `-` its
+  append meaning themselves.
