+
+# Run the TypeScript sources directly on Node's type stripping
+
+## Context
+
+The repository declared `npm test` as `node --test` and a `tsconfig.json` with `declaration` and
+`outDir: dist`, but had no dependencies, no build script and no way to type-check. Something had to
+be settled before the first feature could be tested: either the tests run against compiled output in
+`dist/` (a build step before every test run, and stack traces pointing at generated files), or they
+run against the sources.
+
+Node 22, the version in use, strips TypeScript types on the fly with no flag, so `node --test` can
+run `.ts` tests as they are. The catch is that type stripping does not rewrite import specifiers: a
+module has to import its neighbour as `./json-value.ts`, which `tsc` rejects by default and, if
+emitted verbatim, would produce a `dist/` that cannot be loaded.
+
+## Decision
+
+Tests run against the sources, and `tsconfig.json` is configured to make both halves work:
+
+- `allowImportingTsExtensions` — relative imports carry the `.ts` extension, as Node needs;
+- `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions` — `tsc` turns them back into `.js` when it emits `dist/`;
+- `erasableSyntaxOnly` — rejects the TypeScript syntax that type stripping cannot erase (enums,
+  namespaces, parameter properties), so the sources cannot drift into something Node refuses to run;
+- `verbatimModuleSyntax` — type-only imports must say `import type`, which is what makes erasure
+  unambiguous;
+- `rootDir: src`, which TypeScript 7 asks for explicitly.
+
+`typescript` and `@types/node` are added as `devDependencies`; the library keeps no runtime
+dependency. Three scripts: `test` (unchanged), `typecheck` and `build`.
+
+Type-checking needs a second config, `tsconfig.test.json`: the tests must be checked, but
+`tsconfig.json` keeps `include: ["src"]` so that they stay out of `dist/`. It extends the build
+config with `noEmit`, a `rootDir` of `.` and `types: ["node"]` — the Node types belong to the tests,
+not to the library.
+
+Tests live in `test/`, mirroring the module they cover.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- One command runs the tests, with no build step, and failures point at the real source lines.
+- `npm run typecheck` is the gate that `npm test` is not: type stripping ignores types entirely, so a
+  passing test run says nothing about type errors. Both have to be run before a change is done.
+- The toolchain is pinned to Node 22 or later for development. The published `dist/` is ordinary
+  JavaScript and is not affected.
+- `tsc` is doing the work a linter would otherwise do. If the project adopts one, it should be added
+  alongside, not instead.
