+
+# Implement RFC 6901 JSON Pointer resolvePointer
+
+## Request
+
+Provide a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document: implement JSON Pointer as
+specified in RFC 6901, exporting `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. It returns
+the value the pointer refers to, and throws when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer or does not
+resolve to a value of the document. First of several building blocks; the next ones build on it.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test-first, red before green: `test/json-pointer.test.ts` was written and run against a missing
+module, then the implementation was added until all 43 tests passed.
+
+### Code
+
+- `src/json-value.ts` — the JSON data model (`JsonValue`, `JsonArray`, `JsonObject`), shared by the
+  building blocks to come.
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — `resolvePointer`, the `JsonPointerError` / `InvalidPointerError` /
+  `PointerResolutionError` classes, and the private `parsePointer`, `unescapeToken`, `escapeToken`
+  and `resolveToken` steps.
+- `src/index.ts` — the public surface, re-exporting the function, the three error classes and the
+  three types.
+
+`resolvePointer` validates the pointer against the section 3 grammar first, splits it into reference
+tokens with the escape sequences resolved, then walks the document one token at a time by the section
+4 rules: array indices on arrays, own member names on objects, and a failure on anything else.
+
+### Tests
+
+`test/json-pointer.test.ts`, 43 cases: the twelve examples of RFC 6901 section 5 against the
+document the RFC gives; the empty pointer against composite and non-composite documents; nested
+objects and arrays; the empty member name; escape sequences, including `~01` → `~1` and several
+sequences in one token; and the error paths — invalid syntax, the URI fragment form, a non-string
+pointer, missing members, out-of-range and non-`array-index` tokens, `-` on an array, descending into
+a scalar, and inherited or prototype member names. Two tests pin the behaviour the next building
+blocks rely on: the document is not modified, and the value comes back by reference.
+
+### Toolchain
+
+`tsconfig.json` was adjusted and `tsconfig.test.json` added so that the sources run directly under
+Node's type stripping and still compile to a loadable `dist/`; `typescript` and `@types/node` were
+added as `devDependencies`, with `typecheck` and `build` scripts. See dl-003 for why.
+
+### Documentation
+
+`README.md` gained a "Building blocks" section documenting `resolvePointer`, its two error
+conditions and the own-members-only rule, plus the commands to work on the library.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- dl-001 — the API shape: throwing, with a typed `JsonPointerError` hierarchy, and a shared JSON data
+  model.
+- dl-002 — the strict reading of RFC 6901: no URI fragment form, `-` never resolves, `array-index`
+  enforced, bad indices are resolution and not syntax errors, own members only.
+- dl-003 — running the TypeScript sources directly on Node's type stripping.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 43 tests, 43 pass, 0 fail.
+- `npm run typecheck` — no errors, sources and tests.
+- `npm run build` — emits `dist/`, with relative imports rewritten to `.js`.
+
+## Follow-ups
+
+- No linter is configured; `tsc --strict` is the only static gate today.
+- `package.json` declares no `main` or `exports`, so the built package is not yet consumable as one.
+  Out of scope here, but it has to be settled before another project imports `dist/`.
